<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  RELEASE NETWORK POLICY. This file applies to every build type except debug,
  which supplies its own copy of this resource from src/debug/res/xml.

  Three decisions, all deliberate:

    * cleartextTrafficPermitted="false" with NO domain exceptions. There is no
      host, anywhere, that this application will talk to over plain HTTP in a
      release build. The build configuration is checked independently: the Dart
      configuration loader rejects a non-LOCAL build whose API base URL is not
      https, and rejects a loopback host outside LOCAL.

    * trust-anchors lists ONLY the system store. User-installed certificate
      authorities are excluded, so a certificate added to the device by a proxy,
      an MDM profile or a persuaded user does not become trusted by this app.
      This is the whole reason the file has an explicit trust-anchors block:
      omitting it would inherit the platform default, which trusts user CAs on
      API 23 and below and is silent about intent everywhere else.

    * NO <pin-set>. Certificate pinning is NOT a Phase 4 requirement; the
      architecture decision is on record and revisiting it needs a new ADR, not
      an edit here. TLS is validated by the platform trust store, and the
      transport refuses invalid certificates outright rather than prompting.

  There is deliberately no <debug-overrides> element. A debug-overrides block
  is honoured whenever android:debuggable is true, which couples a security
  decision to a manifest flag that tooling can set. Scoping by source set
  instead means the release resource simply does not contain the exception.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
